LabLink — Master Prompt for Google AI Studio
Optimised for limited credits: one master prompt, gated phases, then short follow-ups.
How to use this (read first)
	•	Open Google AI Studio → Build. Paste Part A (Master Prompt) as your first message. If AI Studio offers to set up Firebase (Auth + Firestore), accept it.
	•	If the output stops early, the prompt makes the AI save a checkpoint file (docs/PROGRESS.md). Paste the Continue prompt from Part B.
	•	When all phases exist, paste the Audit, then Intelligence tests, then UI/3D polish prompts from Part B, one at a time. Do not combine them: smaller prompts waste fewer credits on rewrites.
	•	In the Firebase console enable: Authentication → Email/Password and Google; Firestore database. Add your AI Studio preview domain under Authentication → Settings → Authorized domains.
	•	Set GEMINI_API_KEY as a secret/environment variable in AI Studio. Never paste it in code or chat.

Part A — Master Prompt (copy everything inside the block)
You are a senior full-stack engineer and product designer. Build LabLink, a complete, working, premium full-stack web app. Tagline: "One Campus. Every Lab. Unlimited Possibilities." It is an AI-powered campus laboratory resource-sharing and fair-allocation platform for students, faculty and lab admins. It is for a national innovation challenge: it must be a real, functional product, not a static UI. Make decisions yourself; do not ask questions. Record assumptions in README.md.  === 0. OPERATING RULES (credit efficiency) === - Write each file ONCE, in dependency order. No rewrites, no placeholder files, no TODO stubs for core features. - Do not explain code in chat. Final chat reply: max 15 lines (what works, what needs configuration, how to run tests). - Plan silently, then build in the phase order of section 14. After each phase gate, make sure imports/types compile before continuing. - If you are about to run out of output, stop at a COMPILING state and write docs/PROGRESS.md (done / remaining, by phase and file). Never leave the app broken. - Never fabricate success: no fake logins, fake DB writes, fake AI output or dead buttons. If something needs configuration, render a clear in-app "Configuration required" message with the exact setup step. - Show a visible connection-status chip (Firebase, Firestore, Gemini: Connected / Not configured / Demo mode) in the app shell.  === 1. STACK === React + TypeScript + Vite, Tailwind CSS, React Router, Lucide React, Framer Motion, Recharts, Firebase Auth + Cloud Firestore, @google/genai (Gemini), three + @react-three/fiber + @react-three/drei (3D), Vitest (tests), zod (validation), date-fns. - Gemini is called ONLY from a server route (Express/Node server in the project, e.g. /api/ai/*) using process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY. If the environment has no server runtime, use the environment's secure key mechanism and state this in README. Never hardcode or expose secrets. Keep the Gemini model name in one constant. - Firebase config comes from the file/env AI Studio provisions (or VITE_FIREBASE_* vars). Never hardcode keys. - If Firebase is not configured, run a clearly labelled LOCAL DEMO MODE (localStorage adapter behind the same data-access interface) with a persistent banner and an admin "Reset demo data" button. All data access goes through one repository layer (src/data/repo.ts) with a Firestore implementation and a local-demo implementation.  === 2. DESIGN SYSTEM (premium enterprise SaaS, dark) === Tokens (define as CSS variables + Tailwind theme): bg #080D19, bg2 #0D1424, card #111B2E, elevated #17243A, blue #3B82F6, cyan #22D3EE, violet #8B5CF6, success #10B981, warning #F59E0B, error #EF4444, text #F8FAFC, muted #94A3B8. Fonts: Space Grotesk (headings), Inter (body/controls) via Google Fonts. Card radius 16px, controls 10-12px, thin 1px borders (white at 8% opacity), controlled shadows, glass blur used selectively (sidebar, top bar, modals, hero panels). Consistent 4/8px spacing scale. Visible focus rings, AA contrast, full keyboard navigation, prefers-reduced-motion respected. Shared components (build once, reuse everywhere): AppShell (collapsible sidebar + mobile drawer + top bar with global search, notifications bell with unread count, profile menu, department chip, connection chip), Card, GlassPanel, Button, Input/Select/Textarea, Modal, ConfirmDialog, Toast, DataTable (sort, filter, pagination, mobile card fallback), StatusBadge (booking/request/equipment states with consistent colours), ScoreRing, StatCard, EmptyState, ErrorState, Skeleton, Tabs, Stepper, Calendar/Timeline (day + week), EquipmentCard, PageTransition (subtle fade/translate, 200ms). No excessive glow, no cartoon art, no random decoration, no fake charts, no buttons that do nothing.  === 2B. 3D AND IMAGERY (the visual differentiator; keep it performant) === Use procedural React Three Fiber geometry only (no external model files). Lazy-load every 3D canvas with Suspense + a skeleton; dpr max 1.5; pause rendering when offscreen; WebGL-unsupported or reduced-motion fallback = static gradient image. 1. LANDING HERO: a 3D "connected campus" scene. Floating low-poly lab devices built from primitives (GPU workstation with fan discs and glowing edge lines, 6-axis robotic arm from cylinders/joints, PCB board with chips, 3D-printer frame, oscilloscope) arranged around a cluster of glowing lab nodes joined by thin animated cyan/blue data-link lines (represents "one campus, every lab"). Slow auto-rotate, gentle pointer parallax, navy fog, blue/cyan/violet rim lights. Beside it, an app-preview glass panel (real UI mock built from your components, not an image). Text on the left. 2. EQUIPMENT DETAIL: an interactive 3D viewer (OrbitControls, limited zoom, auto-rotate off on interaction) showing a procedural model chosen by category (GPU workstation, robotic arm, dev board, 3D printer, oscilloscope, sensor, generic instrument box) with a toggle between "3D" and "Photo". 3. EQUIPMENT CARDS: image with navy duotone overlay + CSS 3D pointer-tilt (max 6 degrees, perspective) + category icon + availability badge. Disable tilt on touch/reduced-motion. 4. ADMIN OVERVIEW and LANDING: small "Campus Lab Network" 3D node graph (labs as nodes sized by utilisation, links = cross-department bookings from real data on admin; sample data on landing, labelled "sample"). 5. Images: every equipment record has imageUrl. Use stable seeded photos (https://picsum.photos/seed/{equipmentId}/800/600) or other stable URLs, each with onError fallback to a category SVG illustration. Landing section images use the same approach with navy overlay. All images have alt text and lazy loading.  === 3. DATA MODEL (Firestore) === Collections: users, departments, laboratories, equipment, projects, bookings, bookingRequests, approvals, allocationResults, maintenance, notifications, usageLogs, waitlists, settings, equipmentDaySchedules, auditLogs. Every doc: id, createdAt, updatedAt, status, ownership refs (ownerId/studentId/departmentId etc.), isDemo (true for seeded). Relationships: user->department; laboratory->department; equipment->laboratory+department+responsibleFacultyId; project->student/team; request->project+equipment+student; booking->request+project+equipment; approval->request+facultyId; allocationResult->competing requestIds+weights used+full engine output. Equipment fields: name, equipmentId (e.g. GPU-WS-01), category, description, specs (key/value + numeric fields such as vramGB, cores), departmentId, laboratoryId, building, location, quantity, availableQuantity (derived), condition, maintenanceStatus, responsibleFacultyId, minBookingMins, maxBookingMins, usageRestrictions, requiresCertification, allowsSplitBooking, isActive, imageUrl. CRITICAL CONCURRENCY DESIGN: Firestore client transactions cannot run queries. Therefore keep one document per equipment per day: equipmentDaySchedules/{equipmentId}_{YYYY-MM-DD} containing an array of confirmed intervals {bookingId,start,end,units}. All confirm/approve/reschedule/cancel operations use runTransaction that (1) reads the day-schedule doc(s), (2) re-validates every hard constraint including capacity (concurrent units <= quantity), maintenance, lab hours, max duration, (3) writes booking + schedule + approval + notification docs atomically. If validation fails inside the transaction, abort with a typed error and show the real reason in the UI. Multi-day bookings touch each day doc.  === 4. AUTH, ROLES, SECURITY === - Firebase Auth: email/password register + login, Google sign-in, forgot-password (sendPasswordResetEmail), logout, persistent session, auth-state listener, loading gate, friendly error mapping. - Registration always creates role=student. The register form has an optional "I am faculty/admin" request that writes a roleRequests doc (pending); only an admin approves it in /admin/users. Role is read ONLY from users/{uid} in Firestore, never from the client form or URL. - Role-based routing: ProtectedRoute(role) redirects unauthenticated users to /login, wrong-role users to their own dashboard; post-login redirect by role; navigation items per role. - Demo access (clearly labelled "DEMO ONLY"): on /login show a panel with demo accounts (3 students, 1 faculty, 1 admin, emails @lablink.demo). Clicking one signs in with Firebase (creating the account on first use) and ensures the users doc exists. Firestore rules permit elevated demo roles ONLY when settings/demo.enabled == true AND token email ends with @lablink.demo AND doc.isDemo == true; admin can disable this in /admin/settings. Document in README: production admin is bootstrapped by editing role in Firebase console. - Firestore Security Rules (write firestore.rules, enforce all of this): users can read own profile and cannot change role/departmentApproved fields; students create/read only their own projects, requests, bookings (cancel own only), notifications; students cannot write approvals, allocationResults, bookings with status approved, priority scores or schedules; faculty read requests/equipment for their department/lab and create approvals/transition request status; only faculty/admin write equipmentDaySchedules, and only admin writes equipment, laboratories, departments, maintenance, settings, user roles; auditLogs append-only; priority scores and allocation results are written only by faculty/admin sessions (computed by the deterministic engine, validated by rules for shape and ownership). Validate field types and enum values in rules. Be honest in README about what rules cannot verify (e.g. score recomputation) and how a Cloud Function would harden it. - Validate all input with zod on forms and before writes.  === 5. DETERMINISTIC ALLOCATION ENGINE (src/engine, pure TypeScript, no Firebase/React imports, no LLM) === All functions are pure; take "now" as a parameter (no Date.now inside) so tests are reproducible. 5.1 HARD CONSTRAINTS (checked first; failing requests are INELIGIBLE with machine-readable reason codes, never ranked): EQUIPMENT_INACTIVE, EQUIPMENT_UNAVAILABLE (quantity/available 0), MAINTENANCE_OVERLAP, OUTSIDE_LAB_HOURS, OVERLAPS_CONFIRMED_BOOKING (capacity-aware), EXCEEDS_MAX_DURATION, BELOW_MIN_DURATION, INVALID_TIME_RANGE (end<=start, past), SPEC_MISMATCH (required specs vs equipment specs), ACCESS_NOT_PERMITTED (certification/department restriction), SAFETY_RESTRICTION, DEADLINE_BEFORE_SLOT_END. 5.2 SCORES (each normalised 0-100): - Deadline urgency: hoursToDeadline h; piecewise-linear interpolation through anchors (0h,100) (24h,100) (48h,85) (72h,70) (120h,50) (240h,25) (720h,5); past deadline = 100 and flagged. - Equipment necessity: count qualifying ALTERNATIVE equipment (same category, specs satisfied, active, free in the window) = n; necessity = 100 - 20*min(n,4) (0 alts=100, 1=80, 2=60, 3=40, 4+=20). - Fairness: usedHours = hours used by the requester/team in the last 30 days (usageLogs); fairness = 100*(1 - min(1, usedHours/fairUseLimitHours)); default limit 10h. Exceeding the limit sets flag FAIR_USE_EXCEEDED (soft, unless settings.enforceFairUseHard). Never penalise using another department's equipment or being in a smaller department. - Feasibility: requested slot fully free = 100; else earliest feasible slot before deadline within 24h of requested start = 80, later = 60; only split-booking possible = 40; none before deadline = 0. - Waiting time: waitHours since submission; 100*min(1, waitHours/72). Priority = deadline*w1 + necessity*w2 + fairness*w3 + feasibility*w4 + waiting*w5. Default weights 0.35/0.25/0.20/0.15/0.05; weights come from settings, are auto-normalised to sum 1 and validated 0-1. Return the full per-factor breakdown (raw input, normalised score, weight, weighted contribution). Tie-break: higher deadline score, then lower usage, then earlier submission, then request id. 5.3 ALLOCATION: rank eligible requests; sequentially assign each to a slot: prefer requested slot, else earliest feasible slot (lab hours, outside maintenance, outside confirmed AND already tentatively allocated intervals, within max duration, ends before project deadline). A high score never auto-approves: output is a RECOMMENDATION that faculty must confirm. 5.4 ALTERNATIVES for any unsatisfied/shifted request: top 3 alternative time slots on the same equipment; alternative equipment (same/compatible category, specs met, actually free, ranked by spec match then proximity); nearby-lab resources (same building/campus, labelled); split-booking options when allowsSplitBooking; waitlist option. Each carries a machine-generated reason and uses only real availability data. 5.5 OUTPUT (typed AllocationResult): per request {eligibility, constraintChecks[{code,passed,detail}], scoreBreakdown, rank, recommendedSlot|null, alternatives, flags, reasons[]}, plus conflict groups, weightsUsed, engineVersion, computedAt(now). Persist to allocationResults and auditLogs. 5.6 FAIRNESS: configurable fair-use limit per student/team per rolling window; waitlist candidates use the same fairness score. 5.7 WHAT-IF: runWhatIf(data, weights) clones data, runs the same engine, returns baseline vs simulated ranks/slots and a diff ("B moves 3 -> 2"). It NEVER writes bookings.  === 6. BOOKING FLOW === States: draft, submitted, pending_approval, ai_recommended, approved, rejected, waitlisted, cancelled, completed (valid transition map enforced in code and rules). Features: daily/weekly interactive calendar per equipment (confirmed, maintenance, pending, recommended, free blocks; click/drag to select), start/end validation with live conflict hints, duration limits, project association, confirmation, cancellation (releases schedule atomically), reschedule request, history, waitlist (auto-notify on freed slot, ordered by fairness model). Conflicts are checked client-side (UX), again in the engine, and finally inside the transaction (authoritative). Faculty approval re-validates availability in a transaction; if stale, show "slot no longer available" with fresh alternatives. Faculty actions: approve, reject (reason required), suggest another slot, suggest alternative equipment, request more info. Override of the AI recommendation requires a mandatory reason (min 20 chars); every decision stores facultyId, timestamp, engine recommendation snapshot, and whether it was an override (auditLogs + approvals). Notifications (real Firestore docs created in the same write as the event): request submitted, approved, rejected, alternative suggested, more info requested, booking reminder (computed on login for bookings within 24h), maintenance affecting a booking, waitlist slot freed. Bell with unread count, mark read, notifications page. QR check-in/out: generate QR for approved booking (qrcode lib); faculty/admin verify screen validates ownership + time window; record actual usage in usageLogs on check-out; reject invalid/expired codes. Maintenance: admin schedules/completes maintenance with details and repair history; blocks bookings; if it overlaps existing bookings, flag them and notify affected students.  === 7. GEMINI LAYER (server-side, grounded) === - Explanations: send the server the ACTUAL AllocationResult JSON. Prompt Gemini to explain in plain language using ONLY provided numbers and ids; forbid inventing scores, equipment, slots or confirmations; request structured JSON {summary, perRequest[{requestId, explanation}], citedFactors}. Server validates that every number/id cited exists in the input and rejects/falls back otherwise. On any failure show a deterministic template explanation labelled "Rule-based explanation (Gemini unavailable)". - Natural-language equipment search: Gemini returns a zod-validated filter object (categories, specs thresholds, keywords, time window). The client applies it to stored inventory; the UI shows the extracted filters as editable chips and only real records as results. Fallback: keyword parser, labelled. - LabLink AI assistant (student): supported intents: find equipment by availability/spec, recommend equipment for a project, explain why a request was not allocated (reads real allocationResults), find alternative slot (calls the engine). Retrieval first (query repo/engine), then Gemini phrases the answer from retrieved records only. If nothing matches, say so. - Admin insights: send aggregated stats JSON; Gemini returns insights each tied to a metric key; render metric chips beside each insight; badge "Simulated" when the underlying data isDemo. - Rate-limit and timeout every call; show loading and error states.  === 8. ROUTES AND PAGES (all must work; protected where noted) === PUBLIC: / (landing), /login, /register, /forgot-password. STUDENT: /student/dashboard, /student/equipment, /student/equipment/:id, /student/projects, /student/projects/new, /student/projects/:id, /student/bookings, /student/ai-recommendations, /student/notifications, /student/profile. FACULTY: /faculty/dashboard, /faculty/requests, /faculty/requests/:id, /faculty/calendar, /faculty/equipment, /faculty/allocation (Conflict Resolution Studio + What-If), /faculty/analytics, /faculty/profile. ADMIN: /admin/dashboard, /admin/equipment, /admin/equipment/new, /admin/equipment/:id/edit, /admin/laboratories, /admin/departments, /admin/bookings, /admin/maintenance, /admin/analytics, /admin/users, /admin/settings. LANDING: navbar (Home, Features, How It Works, Equipment, About, Login, Get Started); hero "Your Campus Labs. Connected." with the supporting text from the brief, buttons Explore Resources / Get Started, 3D hero (2B); stats strip computed from real/seed data and labelled sample; 6 feature sections (Unified Discovery, Intelligent Slot Booking, AI Conflict Resolution, Fair Allocation, Faculty Approval, Campus Analytics); How It Works stepper Discover -> Request -> AI Analysis -> Faculty Approval -> Access; 8 equipment category cards; benefits for students/faculty/admins; testimonials only if labelled "Illustrative demo content"; footer. Every link and button navigates or acts. STUDENT: sidebar (Overview, Explore Equipment, My Projects, My Bookings, AI Recommendations, Notifications, Profile, Settings); metrics (available equipment, active bookings, pending approvals, upcoming deadlines); welcome; recommended equipment from real data; upcoming reservations; deadline tracker; recent activity from real events; LabLink AI assistant panel. Equipment discovery: search + filters (department, category, availability, location, specs, condition) + sort + pagination on real records, cross-department visible; detail page with image/3D, specs, lab, faculty, restrictions, availability calendar, similar equipment when unavailable, Request Access (requires a project). Project form: title, description, category, team members, department, equipment requirements, start date, deadline, duration, priority justification, optional attachment (Firebase Storage if configured, else clear message). Project detail: requests, bookings, deadlines, activity history. FACULTY: overview metrics (pending, approved, rejected, occupied now, upcoming); pending-requests table (student, project, department, equipment, slot, deadline, AI score, AI recommendation, status; sort/filter/paginate); request detail with project info, conflicts, score breakdown, explanation, actions; booking calendar; equipment; analytics. ADMIN: overview metrics (total, available, occupied, under maintenance, departments, active bookings, utilisation rate); inventory table with add/edit/deactivate; register laboratories; manage departments; assign responsible faculty; booking restrictions; all bookings; maintenance; users and roles (approve roleRequests); CSV export of reports; settings (weights, fair-use limit, demo toggle, lab hours).  === 9. CONFLICT RESOLUTION STUDIO (/faculty/allocation) — make this the showpiece === A decision-support console (dark, dense, precise, not toy-like), laid out in 3 columns on desktop (stacked on mobile): - Header: conflict group selector (equipment + date), engine version, "Run analysis" button, weights chip, data-as-of time. - Pipeline stepper that animates once on run (about 1.5s total): 1 Hard constraints -> 2 Scoring -> 3 Ranking -> 4 Allocation -> 5 Alternatives -> 6 Explanation. Each step shows its real output when selected. - LEFT: competing request cards ranked by priority; student/team, project, deadline countdown, duration, usage history, ScoreRing with total score, rank badge, eligibility badge. - CENTER: for the selected request, a stacked horizontal breakdown (Deadline/Necessity/Fairness/Feasibility/Waiting) showing raw input, 0-100 score, weight, weighted points (sums exactly to total); a constraint matrix (rows = constraints, columns = requests, pass/fail icons with tooltips of detail); flags (e.g. FAIR_USE_EXCEEDED). - RIGHT/BOTTOM: equipment day timeline (Gantt lane) showing confirmed bookings, maintenance, lab-hour limits, recommended allocation blocks (blue) and alternative blocks (dashed cyan/violet); alternatives tabs (Other slots, Alternative equipment, Nearby labs, Split booking, Waitlist) each with reasons. - Explanation panel: Gemini explanation (with "Generated from engine output" badge and cited factors) or labelled rule-based fallback. - Decision bar: Accept recommendation / Modify (pick slot or equipment) / Override (mandatory reason) / Reject with reason / Suggest alternative; each runs the real transactional flow and logs to approvals + auditLogs. Show a result toast only after the transaction commits. - What-If tab: weight sliders (auto-normalise, reset to default), live re-run on the engine, side-by-side baseline vs simulated ranks/slots with change highlights; banner "Simulation only. No bookings changed." Admin can save weights as default (audited). - Scenario loader (demo only): "Load demo conflict" to seed the reproducible scenario of section 10.  === 10. SEED DATA AND REPRODUCIBLE SCENARIO (all docs isDemo:true, labelled demo) === Departments: AI & Machine Learning; Computer Science & Engineering; Electronics & Communication; Mechanical Engineering; Robotics & Automation. Laboratories: AI Innovation Lab, GPU Computing Lab, Robotics Lab, IoT & Embedded Systems Lab, Electronics Research Lab, Mechanical Design Lab. At least 28 equipment records across all categories (NVIDIA GPU workstations x2, Jetson dev kit, Arduino Mega kits, Raspberry Pi 5, ESP32 IoT kits, ROS2 platform, LiDAR, 3D printers, FPGA boards, oscilloscopes, networking gear, research instruments), some under maintenance, varied utilisation, quantities > 1 for kits. 1 admin, 2 faculty, 5 students, 6+ projects, existing bookings, pending requests, one deliberate conflict, usageLogs over the last 30 days. FIXED DEMO CLOCK: DEMO_NOW = 2026-03-02T09:00 local; scenario day D = 2026-03-03. Equipment GPU-WS-01: quantity 1, lab hours 09:00-18:00, max booking 4h, min 30m, requires CUDA with vramGB>=24. GPU-WS-02 exists but is fully booked on D (alternative that is NOT free). Jetson kit is free but fails the vramGB spec for C, qualifies as a loose alternative for B. On D: confirmed booking 09:00-11:00 (other user), maintenance 15:00-17:00. Free windows: 11:00-15:00 and 17:00-18:00. Day D+1 is fully free. Fair-use limit 10h/30d. Weights default. Student A: Object Detection, deadline DEMO_NOW+24h, needs 2h, requests 11:00-13:00, used 6h in last 30d, submitted 12h before DEMO_NOW. Student B: Image Classification, deadline +120h, 2h, requests 11:00-13:00, used 0h, submitted 48h before. Student C: Research Simulation, deadline +48h, 3h, requests 11:00-14:00, used 11h, submitted 24h before. Expected engine results (verify arithmetic; tolerance 0.1): Deadline A=100, C=85, B=50. Necessity A=100, C=100, B=80 (one loose alternative). Fairness A=40, B=100, C=0 (C flagged FAIR_USE_EXCEEDED, soft). Feasibility A=B=C=100 against confirmed bookings only. Waiting A=16.7, B=66.7, C=33.3. Priority A=83.8, B=75.8, C=71.4 -> rank A, B, C. Allocation: A gets 11:00-13:00; B's requested slot is taken, so B gets 13:00-15:00 (before maintenance, before deadline); C (3h) cannot fit the remaining window, so C gets D+1 09:00-12:00 (before its 48h deadline) plus alternatives listed. This scenario must be loadable by one button and reproduce these numbers exactly.  === 11. ANALYTICS (real records only) === Charts (Recharts, styled to the design tokens, with tooltips, legends, empty states): utilisation by department, most requested equipment, booking demand over time, approved vs rejected, availability, cross-department sharing, maintenance frequency, conflict trends. Filters: date range, department, category, laboratory. Utilisation = occupied approved/completed booking hours clipped to the range, divided by available operating hours (lab hours x days - maintenance hours) x units; show the formula in a tooltip. AI insight section per section 7. Everything recomputes from Firestore data; booking approvals must change these numbers.  === 12. RESPONSIVE AND UX === Desktop-first detailed dashboards; tablet/mobile use a drawer sidebar, stacked cards, tables as cards or horizontal scroll, usable calendar controls, accessible forms. Loading skeletons, empty states with actions, error states with retry, confirm dialogs for destructive actions, toasts only after real success.  === 13. TESTS (Vitest, npm test must pass) === Unit tests on the pure engine and a transaction-like in-memory ScheduleStore: each hard-constraint code; each score function incl. anchors/boundaries; weight normalisation; tie-breaks; the section 10 scenario (exact ranks, scores, slots); fairness (over-limit student does not monopolise); unavailable/inactive/maintenance equipment; alternative equipment/slot suggestions use only free resources; what-if does not mutate input; concurrency: two simultaneous confirmations for the same slot -> exactly one succeeds; capacity-aware overlap for quantity > 1; cancelled booking frees the slot.  === 14. BUILD ORDER WITH GATES === P1 Foundation: tokens, routing, shared components, repo layer, types, zod schemas, engine skeleton. GATE: compiles. P2 Auth: Firebase auth, users doc, roles, ProtectedRoute, login/register/forgot, demo access, rules file. GATE: each role lands on its dashboard. P3 Public site: landing with 3D hero, features, categories. GATE: all links work. P4 Seed + equipment discovery: seed/reset, inventory, filters, detail page with 3D viewer, calendar. P5 Projects, requests, bookings: forms, transactional booking, notifications, faculty approvals. P6 Engine + Conflict Resolution Studio + What-If + Gemini server routes + NL search + assistant. P7 Dashboards (student/faculty/admin) wired to real data, maintenance, QR, waitlist, admin CRUD. P8 Analytics + AI insights + CSV export. P9 Tests, README (setup, env vars, Firebase console steps, deploying rules, assumptions, demo script), final self-audit: no dead buttons, no console errors, build passes.  === 15. ACCEPTANCE (must all work end to end) === Student logs in -> creates a computer-vision project -> searches "NVIDIA GPU workstation" (finds one in another department) -> requests a 2h slot with deadline -> two other students request the same workstation -> engine detects conflict, checks hard constraints, computes scores, recommends slots and alternatives -> Gemini explains from engine output -> faculty opens the request, reviews the studio, approves or modifies -> student gets a notification and confirmed booking -> equipment calendar updates -> admin analytics reflect it -> all data persists after refresh. FINAL: build passes, tests pass, README complete, and the final chat reply lists anything requiring manual configuration.

Part B — Follow-up prompts (paste one at a time, only when needed)
B1. Continue (if the build stopped early)
Read docs/PROGRESS.md and the existing code. Continue the LabLink build from the first incomplete phase, following the original phase order and gates. Do not rewrite or delete working files; edit only what is needed. Write each new file once. No chat explanations. Keep the app compiling at every step. Update docs/PROGRESS.md at the end and reply in max 10 lines.
B2. Functionality audit (after all phases exist)
Perform a complete functionality audit of LabLink. Do not redesign or remove UI. Inspect every route, button, form, card, table, filter and nav item and fix what is broken. Verify: (1) registration and login work with Firebase Auth; (2) role-based routing; (3) equipment search and filters return stored Firestore records; (4) project creation; (5) equipment request submission; (6) booking conflicts detected correctly; (7) priority scores come from the deterministic engine; (8) alternative slots and equipment come from real availability; (9) faculty approve, reject and suggest alternatives; (10) calendars update after approval; (11) admin can add, edit and deactivate equipment; (12) analytics derive from stored records; (13) notifications are created for booking events; (14) Firestore reads and writes persist after refresh. Review firestore.rules, role permissions and booking transactions for concurrency safety. Remove fake success messages, dead buttons, placeholder actions and fabricated AI output; where configuration is missing, show a clear configuration message. Run the build and fix all TypeScript and console errors. Output only a short fix list and anything still needing manual setup.
B3. Intelligence-layer validation and tests
Focus only on the allocation engine and its UI. Audit src/engine against the spec: hard constraints, deadline urgency, necessity, fairness, feasibility, waiting time, alternative equipment, alternative slots, simultaneous booking prevention and explainable results. Ensure the reproducible three-student GPU scenario (A, B, C with the fixed DEMO_NOW clock) produces exactly the expected scores, ranks and slots, and is loadable from the Conflict Resolution Studio. Make sure the What-If simulator lets faculty change weights, auto-normalises them, and previews baseline versus simulated outcomes without writing any bookings. Gemini explanations must be generated only from actual engine output, validated server-side against the engine JSON, and fall back to a labelled rule-based explanation. The studio must visibly show: competing requests, score breakdown, constraint checks, recommended allocation, alternatives and the explanation. Write or complete Vitest tests for scheduling conflicts, capacity-aware overlap, maintenance, unavailable equipment, fairness, what-if immutability and concurrent confirmation (exactly one succeeds). Run the tests and fix failures. Preserve all existing features. Reply in max 10 lines.
B4. UI, 3D and polish pass
Do a professional UI/UX refinement of the whole app. Keep functionality, database structure and auth intact. Keep the design tokens: deep navy, electric blue and cyan accents, Space Grotesk headings, Inter body. Refine: landing page (3D connected-campus hero with parallax and a fallback), login and register, student, faculty and admin dashboards, equipment discovery (3D-tilt cards with duotone images), equipment detail (3D model viewer with 3D/Photo toggle), booking calendar, project details, AI recommendations, analytics and the Conflict Resolution Studio. Ensure consistent spacing, radii, glass surfaces, status colours, sidebar and top bar, table styling, chart styling, skeleton loaders, useful empty states and responsive mobile layouts. Micro-interactions only where they aid comprehension; no excessive animation or decoration. Respect prefers-reduced-motion; lazy-load all 3D; cap pixel ratio; pause offscreen. The Conflict Resolution Studio should read like an advanced decision-support system: ranked request cards with score rings, stacked score breakdown, constraint matrix, equipment day timeline, alternatives tabs, explanation panel and decision bar. Do not change business logic. Run the build and fix errors. Reply in max 8 lines.
Tips to save credits
	•	Run B2, B3 and B4 as separate messages; mixing them causes the AI to rewrite files it just built.
